# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
# Skysay LiveKit self-host — registering SIP sidecar credentials.
#
#   cp .env.registrar.example .env.registrar && chmod 600 .env.registrar
#
# ONLY for the registration topology (a host with no reachable public address).
# A public-IP host still CREATES this file, and leaves the four credential
# values below blank — REGISTRAR, USERNAME, PASSWORD and ALLOWED_ADDRESSES.
# Those four are what select registration mode; blank means "static endpoint",
# which is what you want. The tuning defaults further down (keepalive, expires,
# ports, log level) can stay as shipped: nothing reads them unless the sidecar
# runs. The provision step reads this file to decide which topology to build,
# so a missing file fails that step rather than guessing.
# Do not start the `registrar` profile on a public-IP host.
#
# WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE FILE FROM .env.livekit, and why you should not merge
# them: this file is handed to the sidecar container, and Docker's env_file
# passes the WHOLE file — there is no way to share one file and expose only
# part of it. The sidecar is the most exposed process in the stack: host
# networked, parsing SIP from whatever reaches the socket. Putting the
# control-plane tokens and provider API keys in its environment would turn a
# bug in a SIP parser into a full credential compromise. Nothing from
# .env.livekit belongs here.
#
# NEVER commit the filled-in copy.

# Leave this whole block EMPTY if your host has a public IP that your carrier
# can send INVITEs to — that is the static-endpoint topology and it needs
# nothing here. Fill it in only when your host has NO reachable public address
# (home/office NAT, dynamic IP, CGNAT) and your carrier's trunk is in
# SIP-registration mode. Then start the stack with `--profile registrar`.
# See SELFHOST.md "Choosing your inbound topology".
#
# ALL FOUR of SIP_REGISTRATION_REGISTRAR / _USERNAME / _PASSWORD /
# _ALLOWED_ADDRESSES are required together. Provisioning REFUSES a partially
# filled block rather than guessing: half a registration block would otherwise
# retire the trunk's address allowlist while the sidecar still cannot start.
#
# WHAT:  the carrier host your endpoint REGISTERs to (bare hostname, no scheme
#        and no port — SRV records are followed).
# WHERE: your carrier's registration/"dynamic endpoint" documentation.
# LOOKS: sip.yourcarrier.com
SIP_REGISTRATION_REGISTRAR=

# WHAT:  the REGISTER credentials for that trunk. These are NOT your carrier
#        portal login and NOT the outbound trunk auth — carriers usually
#        generate a dedicated pair when you switch a trunk to registration mode.
# WHERE: the carrier's trunk page for the registration-mode trunk.
SIP_REGISTRATION_USERNAME=
SIP_REGISTRATION_PASSWORD=

# WHAT:  the digest realm the carrier challenges with.
# VALUE: defaults to SIP_REGISTRATION_REGISTRAR, which is correct for most
#        carriers. Set it only if your carrier challenges with a different realm.
#        A carrier that challenges with its bare domain while you register
#        against a regional host needs this set explicitly (DIDWW: always
#        sip.didww.com, even when registering via ams.sip.didww.com). The
#        symptom of a wrong realm is "realms do not match" in the sidecar log
#        while the registration retries forever.
SIP_REGISTRATION_REALM=

# WHAT:  the PUBLIC IPv4 CIDR ranges the carrier may deliver calls FROM. The
#        sidecar rejects INVITEs from anywhere else, so the NAT pinhole cannot
#        be used by a third party to inject calls.
# NOTE:  in registration mode the INVITE may come from the carrier's registrar
#        tier rather than its published "static SIP" signalling nodes, so use a
#        range that covers BOTH rather than only the static-endpoint list.
# ONLY:  public IPv4 CIDRs. A hostname, an IPv6 range, or anything overlapping
#        private/loopback/CGNAT/documentation/reserved space is REJECTED at
#        startup — including 0.0.0.0/0, which would not narrow the boundary but
#        remove it, accepting calls from anyone who can reach the socket.
# NOT A SUBSTITUTE for the host firewall: this guards the sidecar's port, which
#        is the only one the carrier uses. livekit-sip's own 5060/udp must be
#        dropped for non-loopback traffic on BOTH IPv4 and IPv6 — see
#        SELFHOST.md "Required: make 5060/udp loopback-only".
# LOOKS: 203.0.113.0/24,198.51.100.0/28
SIP_REGISTRATION_ALLOWED_ADDRESSES=

# WHAT:  how often the sidecar pokes the carrier to keep the NAT binding alive,
#        in seconds. This is NOT the registration interval: carriers enforce a
#        long minimum registration expiry (often 600s) while NAT UDP bindings
#        expire in 30-120s, so without this keepalive calls stop arriving
#        between refreshes even though the registration still looks healthy.
# VALUE: default 25. Must be between 5 and 28.
SIP_REGISTRATION_KEEPALIVE_SECONDS=25

# WHAT:  how long each registration is valid, in seconds. The carrier may raise
#        this (it answers 423 with its own minimum, e.g. 600).
# VALUE: default 600.
SIP_REGISTRATION_EXPIRES=600

# WHAT:  the UDP port the sidecar listens on, and where it forwards calls.
# VALUE: the defaults put the sidecar on 5062 and livekit-sip on its normal
#        5060, so `sip.yaml` needs no change at all. A non-5060 listen port
#        also side-steps most consumer-router SIP ALGs, which only rewrite
#        traffic they recognise on 5060.
# NOTE:  the target must be an IPv4 address and port, not a hostname — it is
#        also the local-leg entry in the sidecar's source allowlist, so it has
#        to be comparable against a packet's source address.
SIP_REGISTRATION_LISTEN_PORT=5062
SIP_REGISTRATION_TARGET=127.0.0.1:5060

# WHAT:  the publicly reachable `host:port` to advertise in the registration
#        Contact, for the minority of setups where you actually know it — a
#        static port-forward or a 1:1 NAT.
# VALUE: LEAVE EMPTY behind an ordinary NAT. Empty is not a fallback: it is the
#        correct setting, because the address the carrier should use is the one
#        it observes (rport/received), which no local configuration can know.
#        Setting a wrong value here is worse than setting nothing.
# LOOKS: 203.0.113.10:5062
SIP_REGISTRATION_CONTACT_ADDRESS=

# WHAT:  sidecar log verbosity (Kamailio debug level).
# VALUE: default 2 (info). Raise to 3 to trace individual SIP messages.
SIP_REGISTRATION_LOG_LEVEL=2
